00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,040
Alright, this is just a short video on perfusion index and how we can use it in the EMS or

2
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:10,360
at hospital setting.

3
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:13,000
So let's start with a quick overview.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:19,560
Perfusion index is a measurement that's derived from your SPO2 waveform.

5
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:25,440
It basically is a ratio of your pulsating or pulsating blood flow to your non pulsating

6
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:27,080
blood flow.

7
00:00:27,080 --> 00:00:32,200
The values range from very low numbers up to 20%.

8
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:38,200
In general, a higher value indicates your vasculature is dilated and that you have adequate

9
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:43,040
pulsatility or good squeeze from the heart and then lower values would indicate that

10
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:50,320
you have vasoconstriction and or poor pulsatility or poor squeeze.

11
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:56,400
And this is just a screenshot of a ZOL X series monitor showing where you might see the perfusion

12
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:58,840
index on your screen.

13
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:03,260
So the next thing is just in general terms an overview of when we might use the perfusion

14
00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:06,900
index in the EMS or at hospital setting.

15
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:09,560
So I kind of broke this down into two categories.

16
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:16,800
First is guiding resuscitation and the next is assessment of pain.

17
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:20,560
Now I came to this conclusion starting with this narrative review.

18
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:25,280
So this paper just looked at a bunch of the studies that have been done on using perfusion

19
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:30,360
index in a clinical setting and they sort of summarized the various uses here in this

20
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:32,580
next slide.

21
00:01:32,580 --> 00:01:39,080
Of those, I think we can like break them down into two main categories after we eliminate

22
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:41,200
the ones that aren't relevant in our setting.

23
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:46,120
So again, that would be guiding resuscitation and assessment of pain.

24
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:50,600
Now along the way I did find another narrative review which I think is actually a little

25
