00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:54,720
So that is shown here.

27
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:58,600
So if you were looking for one paper to kind of get up to speed on the use of perfusion

28
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:04,760
index in a clinical setting in general, I think this paper is the place to start.

29
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:10,040
Now in terms of perfusion index and resuscitation, we can kind of break this down into a couple

30
00:02:10,040 --> 00:02:11,040
different ideas.

31
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:15,920
So the first is we can use perfusion index to determine whether we need to resuscitate

32
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:16,920
or not.

33
00:02:16,920 --> 00:02:22,840
We can also evaluate the efficacy of our resuscitation and then specifically we can use a perfusion

34
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:26,560
index to evaluate fluid responsiveness.

35
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:30,700
Now I'm not going to get into the details on the specific studies but I'll just show

36
