00:03:41,380 --> 00:03:47,020
So there is some data for using perfusion index both in resuscitation and for pain assessment.

53
00:03:47,020 --> 00:03:51,060
So let's just summarize the objective side of these things.

54
00:03:51,060 --> 00:03:55,140
In general low numbers are not ideal.

55
00:03:55,140 --> 00:03:59,900
Average like what is normal is also a bit elusive maybe in the 1 to 5 range and then

56
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:05,460
there is an idea that high numbers so over 10 maybe over 15 could be associated with

57
00:04:05,460 --> 00:04:10,900
some sort of pathology or pathophysiology but that's unlikely to be a cause for acute

58
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:12,940
concern.

59
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:18,940
For using perfusion index to guide resuscitation consider very low numbers such as under 0.5

60
00:04:18,940 --> 00:04:24,420
to indicate you might need pressors of course along with other resuscitative interventions.

61
00:04:24,420 --> 00:04:29,260
Below 1 or below 1.5 consider resuscitation and then if you have a notable increase in

62
00:04:29,260 --> 00:04:34,020
perfusion index with fluids that may indicate that you can keep repeating that your patient

63
00:04:34,020 --> 00:04:37,220
is fluid responsive.

64
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:41,520
And then in terms of pain we should see a decreasing perfusion index with an increase

65
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:47,400
in pain and then an increasing perfusion index with effective analgesia.

66
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:52,160
So a low perfusion index means we need to give more drugs and a higher perfusion index

67
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:54,860
means we have adequate analgesia.

68
00:04:54,860 --> 00:04:59,040
And then we do also want to coordinate this with other indicators of pain so normal pain

69
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:03,160
incidation scales, vital signs that sort of thing.

70
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:09,280
So that's just a quick overview of perfusion index in a clinical setting but our setting

71
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:12,480
in the out of hospital world is a little bit different.

72
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:15,440
So how does perfusion index work in our setting?

73
00:05:15,440 --> 00:05:20,240
So there's one of these papers that we mentioned previously that basically summarized all of

74
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:25,960
the factors that could contribute to perfusion index being less reliable.

75
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:29,240
And then I've highlighted all the ones in yellow that apply in our setting.

76
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:35,480
So if we look at this list we have things like patient positioning, vascular compression,

77
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:38,120
movement, external lights, etc.

78
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:45,080
All of this stuff decreases the reliability of perfusion index and it decreases the correlation

79
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:54,560
between perfusion index as an objective parameter and either pain assessment or evaluating resuscitation.

80
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:59,840
So if we do choose to use perfusion index in the out of hospital setting we just want

81
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:03,320
to be cognizant of these things and do what we can to control them.

82
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:07,640
Okay so let's end with a quick summary.

83
00:06:07,640 --> 00:06:11,720
One thing we didn't mention before is that it's totally fine to use a perfusion index

84
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:15,080
to verify the accuracy of your SPO2 tracing.

85
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:21,160
This is one of the initial indications or utilizations of perfusion index.

86
00:06:21,160 --> 00:06:28,200
Moving on from that in general normal is highly variable parameter but maybe in the 1 to 5%

87
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:32,760
range but trending is probably more valuable over time versus just a single snapshot or

88
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:34,720
single value.

89
00:06:34,720 --> 00:06:40,920
Low values so less than 0.5 or less than 1 indicate potentially solvable issues.

90
00:06:40,920 --> 00:06:46,600
For example like making sure the measurement is happening correctly, resuscitate, get fluids,

91
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:49,680
issue pressors, street pain, that sort of thing.

92
00:06:49,680 --> 00:06:57,400
High values so over 10 maybe over 15 are probably fine but may correlate with some sort of hyperdynamic

93
00:06:57,400 --> 00:06:59,280
pathophysiology.

94
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:05,320
Most of this would be early sepsis, high output, heart failure, unmarked, sympathetic

95
00:07:05,320 --> 00:07:08,440
response and trauma, that sort of thing.

96
00:07:08,440 --> 00:07:09,440
And that's it.

